2. SpringBootJdbcApplication — the starting point
@SpringBootApplication
public class SpringBootJdbcApplication {

@SpringBootApplication tells Spring Boot:

This is the main application. Start Spring from here.

It also enables component scanning. Since your other classes are inside subpackages of:

com.JDBC2.SpringBootJDBC

Spring can find:

com.JDBC2.SpringBootJDBC.Model.Alien
com.JDBC2.SpringBootJDBC.Repo.AlienRepo
Starting Spring
ApplicationContext context =
    SpringApplication.run(SpringBootJdbcApplication.class, args);

This line starts the entire Spring Boot application.

ApplicationContext

Think of it as a container/warehouse managed by Spring.

Spring scans your project and creates objects called beans.

After this line runs, conceptually:

ApplicationContext
│
├── Alien bean
├── AlienRepo bean
├── JdbcTemplate bean
├── DataSource bean
└── many other Spring beans
3. Alien class — your Model
@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class Alien {
@Component

This tells Spring:

"Please manage objects of this class."

So Alien becomes a Spring bean.

@Scope("prototype")

This is particularly important.

By default, Spring beans are:

singleton

Meaning:

context.getBean(Alien.class)
        ↓
same object


context.getBean(Alien.class)
        ↓
same object again

But you used:

@Scope("prototype")

Now every request gives you a new Alien object:

Alien alien1 = context.getBean(Alien.class);
Alien alien2 = context.getBean(Alien.class);

Conceptually:

alien1 ──► Alien object #1
alien2 ──► Alien object #2

So:

Alien alien = context.getBean(Alien.class);

asks Spring:

Give me an Alien object.

Setting the values
alien.setId(1);
alien.setName("Alien");
alien.setTech("java");

Now your Java object contains:

Alien object
├── id   = 1
├── name = "Alien"
└── tech = "java"

At this point, nothing has been saved to the database yet.

This is just a Java object in memory.

4. AlienRepo — Database layer
@Repository
public class AlienRepo {

@Repository tells Spring:

This class handles database operations.

It also makes AlienRepo a Spring-managed bean.

So you can do:

AlienRepo repo = context.getBean(AlienRepo.class);

Conceptually:

ApplicationContext
       │
       └── gives you ──► AlienRepo object
5. JdbcTemplate

Inside AlienRepo:

private JdbcTemplate temp;

JdbcTemplate is a Spring JDBC helper.

Without JdbcTemplate, traditional JDBC looks something like:

Connection
    ↓
Statement / PreparedStatement
    ↓
Execute SQL
    ↓
Process ResultSet
    ↓
Close connection

With JdbcTemplate, Spring handles much of this for you.

You mainly write:

SQL + values
Dependency Injection

You wrote:

@Autowired
public void setTemp(JdbcTemplate temp) {
    this.temp = temp;
}

This is setter injection.

Conceptually:

Spring creates AlienRepo
        │
        ▼
Spring sees @Autowired
        │
        ▼
"AlienRepo needs JdbcTemplate"
        │
        ▼
Spring provides JdbcTemplate
        │
        ▼
temp = JdbcTemplate object

So your repository becomes:

AlienRepo
    │
    └── temp ──► JdbcTemplate
                       │
                       └── H2 Database
6. What happens with schema.sql

Your schema.sql contains:

create table alien (
 id int primary key,
 name varchar(50),
 tech varchar(50)
);

When Spring Boot starts, it finds schema.sql and executes it.

So:

Spring Boot starts
      │
      ▼
Finds schema.sql
      │
      ▼
Executes CREATE TABLE
      │
      ▼
H2 Database
      │
      ▼
alien table created

Your table becomes conceptually:

id	name	tech

7. What happens with data.sql

Your data.sql contains:

insert into alien (id, name, tech) values (101, 'Kiran', 'Blockchain');


insert into alien (id, name, tech) values (102, 'Shubham', 'Java');


insert into alien (id, name, tech) values (103, 'Rahul', 'Spring Boot');

Spring Boot executes these after the table has been created.

The database now contains:

id	name	tech
101	Kiran	Blockchain
102	Shubham	Java
103	Rahul	Spring Boot
8. Your Java code adds one more Alien

You create:

Alien alien = context.getBean(Alien.class);


alien.setId(1);
alien.setName("Alien");
alien.setTech("java");

Now:

Java memory:


Alien
├── id = 1
├── name = Alien
└── tech = java

Then:

repo.save(alien);

calls:

public void save(Alien alien) {
    String sql = "insert into alien(id,name,tech) values(?,?,?)";


    int rows = temp.update(
        sql,
        alien.getId(),
        alien.getName(),
        alien.getTech()
    );


    System.out.print(rows);
}
9. Understanding ?

Your SQL is:

insert into alien(id,name,tech) values(?,?,?)

The ? are placeholders.

These values:

alien.getId(),
alien.getName(),
alien.getTech()

replace them in order:

First ?  → 1
Second ? → "Alien"
Third ?  → "java"

Conceptually, the database receives:

insert into alien(id,name,tech)
values(1, 'Alien', 'java');

So your table now becomes:

id	name	tech
101	Kiran	Blockchain
102	Shubham	Java
103	Rahul	Spring Boot
1	Alien	java
10. What is temp.update()?
int rows = temp.update(sql, ...);

update() is generally used for SQL operations that modify data:

INSERT
UPDATE
DELETE

It returns the number of affected rows.

Since one row was inserted:

rows = 1

Therefore:

System.out.print(rows);

prints:

1

Meaning:

One row was successfully affected.

11. findAll() — reading the data

Now you call:

System.out.println(repo.findAll());

Inside findAll():

String sql = "select * from alien";

This means:

Get every row from the alien table.

The database result is conceptually:

ResultSet
│
├── Row 1 → 101, Kiran, Blockchain
├── Row 2 → 102, Shubham, Java
├── Row 3 → 103, Rahul, Spring Boot
└── Row 4 → 1, Alien, java

But the database gives you rows. Your Java application wants:

List<Alien>

So we need a conversion.

That is the job of RowMapper.

12. What is RowMapper<Alien>?
RowMapper<Alien> rowMapper = new RowMapper<Alien>() {

A RowMapper says:

"For every database row, tell me how to convert it into an Alien Java object."

Your code:

public Alien mapRow(ResultSet rs, int rowNum) throws SQLException {
    Alien alien = new Alien();


    alien.setId(rs.getInt("id"));
    alien.setName(rs.getString("name"));
    alien.setTech(rs.getString("tech"));


    return alien;
}

For this database row:

id     = 101
name   = Kiran
tech   = Blockchain

You create:

Alien object
├── id = 101
├── name = Kiran
└── tech = Blockchain

This happens for every row.

Conversion process
DATABASE                          JAVA
────────                          ────


Row 1 ───────────────────────► Alien object #1
101, Kiran, Blockchain


Row 2 ───────────────────────► Alien object #2
102, Shubham, Java


Row 3 ───────────────────────► Alien object #3
103, Rahul, Spring Boot


Row 4 ───────────────────────► Alien object #4
1, Alien, java

Finally:

temp.query(sql, rowMapper);

returns:

List<Alien>
13. Why does printing the list work nicely?

You overrode:

@Override
public String toString()

Without it, Java would print something ugly like:

[com.JDBC2.SpringBootJDBC.Model.Alien@1a2b3c]

But because you have:

@Override
public String toString() {
    return "Alien{" +
            "id=" + id +
            ", name='" + name + '\'' +
            ", tech='" + tech + '\'' +
            '}';
}

You get something readable:

[
 Alien{id=101, name='Kiran', tech='Blockchain'},
 Alien{id=102, name='Shubham', tech='Java'},
 Alien{id=103, name='Rahul', tech='Spring Boot'},
 Alien{id=1, name='Alien', tech='java'}
]
Complete execution flow

This is the most important thing to remember:

1. main() starts
        ↓
2. SpringApplication.run()
        ↓
3. Spring Boot starts ApplicationContext
        ↓
4. Spring finds @Component Alien
        ↓
5. Spring finds @Repository AlienRepo
        ↓
6. Spring configures H2 database + JdbcTemplate
        ↓
7. schema.sql runs
        ↓
   alien table is created
        ↓
8. data.sql runs
        ↓
   Kiran, Shubham, Rahul inserted
        ↓
9. Get Alien bean from Spring
        ↓
10. Set id, name, tech
        ↓
11. Get AlienRepo from Spring
        ↓
12. repo.save(alien)
        ↓
13. JdbcTemplate executes INSERT
        ↓
14. New Alien is saved
        ↓
15. repo.findAll()
        ↓
16. SELECT * FROM alien
        ↓
17. RowMapper converts each row → Alien object
        ↓
18. List<Alien> returned
        ↓
19. System.out.println() prints the list
One important distinction
@Component / @Repository
        ↓
Spring manages your Java objects


JdbcTemplate
        ↓
Communicates with the database


schema.sql
        ↓
Creates database structure


data.sql
        ↓
Provides initial database data


RowMapper
        ↓
Converts database rows → Java objects
